  "title": "The arras contract decides what you bought",
  "source_article": "property-contracts.txt, buying-10-steps.txt, bank-guarantees.txt",
  "post_text": "The arras draft lands in your inbox two days before signing day. That window is not a courtesy. It is your only real chance to change the contract.\n\nMost buyers in Spain check the price and skim the rest. But the arras decides what you actually bought.\n\nThe agency usually adapts a standard contract, filling in the property details as they appear on the Nota Simple. Beyond price and description, it should fix:\n\nThe completion date, usually two to three months after signing, enough time for your documents and mortgage.\nThe penalties: pull out and you lose your 10 percent deposit; if the seller pulls out, they pay you double.\nThe specifics: which furniture stays, which repairs the seller pays for, whether appliances are included, whether water and electricity will be switched on.\n\nIf it is not written in the arras, it was never agreed.\n\nNow the money. That 10 percent often goes directly to the owners. Common in Spain, but a safeguarded account at a financial institution is the safer route. Ask for it.\n\nBuying a new build? Add completion date clauses and insist on the bank guarantee that protects staged payments.\n\nSo here is the habit: read the draft the day it arrives, line by line, and send your amendments before signing day. At the table it is too late.\n\nGeneral information, not legal advice.\n\n#SpainProperty #BuyingInSpain #MoveToSpain",
