  "title": "Spain taxes your empty holiday home",
  "source_article": "irnr.txt, non-resident-property-tax.txt",
  "post_text": "Your Spanish holiday home earned nothing this year. Spain will still tax you on it. The trap is that no bill ever arrives to remind you.\n\nFirst, work out which side of the line you are on. Spain treats you as a tax resident if any of these is true:\n\nYou spent more than 183 days in Spain this calendar year, continuous or not.\nYour spouse and underage children live there.\nThe majority of your income and economic activity is in Spain.\n\nNone of those? Then you are a non-resident, and IRNR applies simply for owning property. Spain imputes income at 1.1 percent of the cadastral value, or 2 percent in municipalities without a recent cadastral revision, then taxes it at 19 percent if you live in the EU or EEA, 24 percent for everyone else.\n\nReal numbers: on a 300,000 euro property at 1.1 percent, an owner in Norway pays 19 percent of 3,300 euros. About 627 euros a year.\n\nThe filing is Modelo 210, due by 31 December, and it always covers the previous year. Rent the place out and the rhythm changes to quarterly.\n\nIgnore it long enough and the escalation is real: fines, closure of bank accounts, even confiscation of property in extreme cases.\n\nThis week: count your days in Spain, and check whether last year's Modelo 210 was actually filed. Many owners assume someone else did it.\n\nGeneral information, not tax advice.\n\n#SpanishTax #Modelo210 #HolidayHome",
