  "title": "The 48-hour squatter rule",
  "source_article": "squatter-laws.txt",
  "post_text": "Spanish law gives you 48 hours to remove squatters without a court. Most owners lose that window before they even know it opened.\n\nInside 48 hours of entry, police can evict occupiers immediately if you can prove the property is yours. Past that, Spain's complicated squatting law takes over.\n\nWhy complicated? The law splits squatting into two figures: allanamiento de morada, breaking into an inhabited home, and okupacion, occupying an uninhabited one. What counts as uninhabited is blurry for a second home, and Article 47 of the Constitution declares a right to decent housing. That mix is why evictions take time.\n\nOutside the window, it becomes a court case. You prove ownership and regular use: deeds, photos of you in the home, flight tickets, neighbour statements. The squatters can argue their own rights or show they are paying to stay. Only when a judge sets the date can police remove them by force. Depending on the region, 18 months to 2 years, though many resolve faster.\n\nAnd the counterintuitive part: do not change the locks, block re-entry or confront anyone. All of it can backfire on you legally.\n\nWhat protects you is detection speed. An alarm connected to the police deters entry and timestamps it, keeping you inside the 48 hours. A neighbour who checks the property does the same. Some insurers even offer illegal occupation cover.\n\nSet up the alarm and the neighbour before the season you are away. Not after.\n\nGeneral information, not legal advice.\n\n#SpainProperty #SpanishHomeOwners #HolidayHome",
