  "title": "Cash in Spain now needs paperwork",
  "source_article": "cash-rules.txt",
  "post_text": "Want to withdraw 3,000 euros in cash from your Spanish bank tomorrow? You needed to tell the tax office today.\n\nSince 2025, withdrawals above 3,000 euros, or any amount involving 500 euro notes, must be reported to the Agencia Tributaria 24 hours in advance. You notify via their website with proof of ID, sometimes proof of where the funds came from, and receive a certificate to present at the bank. From 100,000 euros, the notice period becomes 72 hours.\n\nPaying in cash has its own ceilings: 1,000 euros for any transaction involving a business, 1,000 between private individuals, and 10,000 if you are a non-resident who does not pay tax in Spain.\n\nTransfers get paperwork too. Any cash transfer within Spain over 100,000 euros, property purchases included, must be declared on the S1 form. Sending more than 10,000 euros to someone requires documentation proving the origin of the funds.\n\nBreak the rules and fines run from up to 50 percent for minor infractions to 150 percent where fraud is involved. One softener: if payer or payee self-reports within three months, they may be exempt.\n\nCrossing the border with more than 10,000 euros in cash? Declare it, or risk a fine of up to half the undeclared amount.\n\nSo if you are buying, furnishing or renovating: plan on transfers, not envelopes, and keep receipts for large transactions for at least five years. Questions can come long after the money moved.\n\nGeneral information, not tax advice.\n\n#BankingInSpain #SpanishTax #ExpatSpain",
