  "title": "The 100% tax: the real numbers",
  "source_article": "noneu-100-tax.txt",
  "post_text": "\"Spain will charge non-EU buyers 100 percent tax.\" Before you shelve your plans over that headline, look at the numbers underneath it.\n\nYes, Spain's Prime Minister has proposed a 100 percent tax on property purchases by non-EU residents: anyone spending under 183 days a year in Spain, which includes UK and US buyers of second homes.\n\nThe context: it is one of twelve measures against a housing crisis in which prices have nearly doubled in a decade, and rents in Madrid and Barcelona rose by as much as 33 percent in five years. It landed alongside the closing of the Golden Visa on 3 April 2025, which ended residency through 500,000 euro property purchases.\n\nNow the scale nobody quotes. In 2023, foreigners bought 15 percent of Spanish properties: 87,000 of 583,000 sales. Non-EU non-residents? 27,000, about 5 percent, mostly from the US, UK, Morocco, Venezuela and Mexico, and mostly coastal holiday homes rather than the city flats at the centre of the crisis.\n\nFor perspective on today's reality: in Valencia, residents and non-residents alike currently pay the same 10 percent transfer tax.\n\nAnd the detail that matters most: this remains a proposal under study. There is no final text, no date, no mechanism.\n\nSo watch the legislation, by all means. But a 5 percent slice of the market is the thing being debated. Do not restructure your life around a headline.\n\nGeneral information, not legal advice.\n\n#SpainProperty #BuyingInSpain #MoveToSpain",
